# 6. Azure Table Storage — MenuItems

The menu is stored using **Azure Table Storage**.

The table is called:

```text
MenuItems
```

Each menu item contains:

| Property     | Description                             |
| ------------ | --------------------------------------- |
| PartitionKey | Menu category                           |
| RowKey       | Unique menu item ID/SKU                 |
| Name         | Name of the coffee/menu item            |
| Description  | Description of the item                 |
| Price        | Price of the item                       |
| IsAvailable  | Whether the item is currently available |
| Timestamp    | Azure Table timestamp                   |
| ETag         | Entity version/concurrency value        |

### Example entity

```json
{
    "partitionKey": "Hot Drinks",
    "rowKey": "COF-001",
    "name": "Espresso",
    "description": "Strong freshly brewed espresso",
    "price": 25.00,
    "isAvailable": true
}
```

---

# 7. Menu API Endpoints

The CoffeeNChill API provides the following menu operations.

| Method | Endpoint                        | Purpose                         |
| ------ | ------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| POST   | `/api/menu`                     | Create a menu item              |
| GET    | `/api/menu`                     | Retrieve all menu items         |
| GET    | `/api/menu/category/{category}` | Retrieve menu items by category |
| PUT    | `/api/menu/{category}/{id}`     | Update a menu item              |
| DELETE | `/api/menu/{category}/{id}`     | Delete a menu item              |

---

# 8. Create Menu Item

### Request

```http
POST http://localhost:7071/api/menu
```

### Body

```json
{
    "partitionKey": "Hot Drinks",
    "rowKey": "COF-001",
    "name": "Espresso",
    "description": "Strong freshly brewed espresso",
    "price": 25.00,
    "isAvailable": true
}
```

### Successful response

```text
201 Created
```

The created menu item is stored in the `MenuItems` Azure Table.

---

# 9. Get All Menu Items

### Request

```http
GET http://localhost:7071/api/menu
```

### Successful response

```text
200 OK
```

Example:

```json
[
    {
        "partitionKey": "Hot Drinks",
        "rowKey": "COF-001",
        "name": "Espresso",
        "description": "Strong freshly brewed espresso",
        "price": 25.00,
        "isAvailable": true
    },
    {
        "partitionKey": "Cold Drinks",
        "rowKey": "COF-002",
        "name": "Iced Coffee",
        "description": "Cold coffee served with ice",
        "price": 30.00,
        "isAvailable": true
    }
]
```

---

# 10. Get Menu Items by Category

### Request

```http
GET http://localhost:7071/api/menu/category/Hot%20Drinks
```

This endpoint retrieves menu items belonging to a specific category.

Example category:

```text
Hot Drinks
```

Expected response:

```text
200 OK
```

---

# 11. Update Menu Item

### Request

```http
PUT http://localhost:7071/api/menu/Hot%20Drinks/COF-001
```

The update functionality allows menu information such as pricing and availability to be changed.

Example:

```json
{
    "price": 28.00,
    "isAvailable": false
}
```

Expected response:

```text
200 OK
```

---

# 12. Delete Menu Item

### Request

```http
DELETE http://localhost:7071/api/menu/Hot%20Drinks/COF-001
```

This removes the selected menu item from Azure Table Storage.

Expected response:

```text
204 No Content
```

---

# 13. Azure File Share — Staff Documents

Staff documents are stored using an Azure File Share.

The file share is:

```text
staff-docs
```

Example documents include:

```text
staff-docs/
│
├── barista-recipes.pdf
├── cleaning-manual.pdf
├── health-and-safety.pdf
└── equipment-guide.pdf
```

---

# 14. Upload Staff Document

### Request

```http
POST http://localhost:7071/api/documents/upload
```

The request uses:

```text
multipart/form-data
```

The uploaded file is stored inside the `staff-docs` Azure File Share.

---

# 15. List Staff Documents

### Request

```http
GET http://localhost:7071/api/documents
```

The endpoint returns the files currently stored in the staff document share.

Example response:

```json
[
    {
        "name": "barista-recipes.pdf",
        "size": 15234
    },
    {
        "name": "cleaning-manual.pdf",
        "size": 28342
    }
]
```

---

# 16. Download Staff Document

### Request

```http
GET http://localhost:7071/api/documents/download/barista-recipes.pdf
```

The requested file is returned to the client.

---

# 17. Azurite Local Storage

Azurite is used to emulate Azure Storage during local development.

The official Azurite Docker image is:

```text
mcr.microsoft.com/azure-storage/azurite
```

The project uses the following storage ports:

| Service       |  Port |
| ------------- | ----: |
| Blob Storage  | 10000 |
| Queue Storage | 10001 |
| Table Storage | 10002 |

---

# 18. Running Azurite

Start the Azurite container using:

```powershell
docker run -d `
  --name coffeenchill-azurite `
  -p 10000:10000 `
  -p 10001:10001 `
  -p 10002:10002 `
  mcr.microsoft.com/azure-storage/azurite
```

Check the running containers:

```powershell
docker ps
```

Expected result:

```text
CONTAINER ID   IMAGE                                     STATUS
xxxxxxxx       mcr.microsoft.com/azure-storage/azurite   Up
```

The container name used by the project is:

```text
coffeenchill-azurite
```

---

# 19. Verify Azurite

To view the Azurite logs:

```powershell
docker logs coffeenchill-azurite
```

To stop Azurite:

```powershell
docker stop coffeenchill-azurite
```

To start the existing container again:

```powershell
docker start coffeenchill-azurite
```

To remove the container:

```powershell
docker rm -f coffeenchill-azurite
```

---

# 20. Local Settings

For local development, Azure Functions uses the following storage configuration:

```json
{
    "IsEncrypted": false,
    "Values": {
        "AzureWebJobsStorage": "UseDevelopmentStorage=true",
        "FUNCTIONS_WORKER_RUNTIME": "dotnet-isolated"
    }
}
```

This configuration allows the application to use Azurite instead of requiring an Azure Storage account during local development.

> `local.settings.json` should not contain real production secrets and should not be committed if it contains sensitive credentials.

---

# 21. Running the Azure Functions Project

Open PowerShell in the CoffeeNChill project directory:

```powershell 
cd "C:\Users\elroi_svxj6s\source\repos\CoffeeNChill\CoffeeNChill"
```

Start the Azure Functions application:

```powershell
func start
```

The Functions host should start on:

```text
http://localhost:7071
```

The API can then be tested using Postman.

---

# 22. Postman Testing

Postman was used to test all HTTP endpoints.

The recommended environment variable is:

```text
baseUrl = http://localhost:7071
```

This allows requests to use:

```text
{{baseUrl}}/api/menu
```

instead of repeatedly typing the complete URL.

---

